         .canvas-wrap { position: relative; }
         
         @media (max-width: 480px){
@@ -468,6 +480,73 @@
 
     <p>Read it term by term. $W$ is the original frozen weight, shape $d \times k$. $A$ is $r \times k$ and $B$ is $d \times r$, where the rank $r \ll d$ is tiny compared to the weight dims. Only $A$ and $B$ are trained. $W$ never moves.</p>
 
+    <p>This is the actual diagram from the LoRA paper. Not my redrawing, the authors' own Figure 1. The whole method is in this one picture: the blue box is the weight you already have, frozen solid, and the two orange slabs are the only thing you train. Tap the numbered markers and I will walk you through it.</p>
+
+    <div class="demo" id="loraPaperDemo">
+      <div style="position:relative;max-width:640px;margin:0 auto;">
+        <img src="figures/papers/lora-fig1.png" alt="Figure 1 from the LoRA paper: input x at the bottom feeds a frozen blue box of pretrained weights W on the left and two stacked orange parallelograms labeled A and B on the right, with a rank r brace between them; both paths combine at a plus sign into output h at the top." style="width:100%;height:auto;display:block;">
+        <button type="button" class="lora-hotspot" data-h="0" style="position:absolute;left:22%;top:42%;transform:translate(-50%,-50%);" aria-label="Marker 1: the frozen pretrained weight">1</button>
+        <button type="button" class="lora-hotspot" data-h="1" style="position:absolute;left:75%;top:66%;transform:translate(-50%,-50%);" aria-label="Marker 2: A, the down-projection">2</button>
+        <button type="button" class="lora-hotspot" data-h="2" style="position:absolute;left:74%;top:32%;transform:translate(-50%,-50%);" aria-label="Marker 3: B, the up-projection">3</button>
+        <button type="button" class="lora-hotspot" data-h="3" style="position:absolute;left:75%;top:50%;transform:translate(-50%,-50%);" aria-label="Marker 4: the rank r bottleneck">4</button>
+        <button type="button" class="lora-hotspot" data-h="4" style="position:absolute;left:50%;top:14%;transform:translate(-50%,-50%);" aria-label="Marker 5: the plus merge">5</button>
+      </div>
+      <p class="diag-label hotspot-text" id="loraHotspotText">Tap a numbered marker on the figure.</p>
+      <p class="paper-caption"><strong>Figure 1.</strong> Our reparametrization. We only train $A$ and $B$. Rebuilt from Figure 1 of Hu et al., "LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models," <em>ICLR</em> 2022. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685">arXiv:2106.09685</a></p>
+      <p class="paper-citation">Full citation: Hu, E. J., Shen, Y., Wallis, P., Allen-Zhu, Z., Li, Y., Wang, S., Wang, L., and Chen, W. <em>LoRA: Low-Rank Adaptation of Large Language Models.</em> ICLR 2022. <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2106.09685">arXiv:2106.09685</a></p>
+      <div class="demo-controls">
+        <button type="button" class="play-btn" id="loraPaperPlay">Play</button>
+      </div>
+      <div class="cap-card">
+        <strong>How to play</strong>
+        <ul>
+          <li><strong>The point:</strong> This is the actual Figure 1 from the LoRA paper, and it is the entire method in one drawing: one frozen path, one trainable side path, added together. The page's equation $W_{\text{LoRA}} = W + B \cdot A$ is literally this picture.</li>
+          <li><strong>What you'll see:</strong> The five markers light up one by one along the signal path, about half a second apart: the frozen weight, the down-projection A, the up-projection B, the rank bottleneck r, then the plus sign where the two paths merge into h. Each lit marker brings a one-line explanation of the part it sits on.</li>
+          <li class="try-it"><strong>Try this:</strong> tap 2 and 3 back to back and read the init story (random A, zero B, so the model starts exactly pretrained). Then tap 5 and compare with the bottleneck adapters above on this page: those stay in the graph forever, LoRA's side path melts away.</li>
+        </ul>
+      </div>
+    </div>
+
+    <script>
+    (function () {
+        var demo = document.getElementById('loraPaperDemo');
+        if (!demo) return;
+        var btns = demo.querySelectorAll('.lora-hotspot');
+        var box = document.getElementById('loraHotspotText');
+        var playBtn = document.getElementById('loraPaperPlay');
+        if (!btns.length || !box) return;
+        var texts = [
+            '1. The frozen W path. The blue box is the pretrained weight W. The drawing shows it square, d by d; the paper writes it more generally as d by k. It is frozen: no gradients, no optimizer state, nothing moves. The input x feeds straight through it, exactly as it did before fine-tuning. The d brace at the bottom marks the input width.',
+            '2. A, the down-projection. A is r by k, the first skinny matrix. It starts from a random Gaussian, so on step one it spits out noise. That would be a problem, except B starts at zero (marker 3), which zeroes the whole product out.',
+            '3. B, the up-projection. B is d by r, initialized to exactly zero. Because B is zero at the start, the update BA is zero, so on step one the model behaves exactly like the pretrained model. Training starts from do nothing, not from random damage.',
+            '4. The r annotation. r is the rank, the width of the bottleneck between A and B. This is the entire budget of new parameters: A and B together hold r times (d + k) numbers, against d times k in the full weight. On GPT-3 175B, where d is 12,288, the paper found r = 1 or 2 was already enough.',
+            '5. The merge. The plus is the forward pass: h = Wx + BAx. Both paths see the same input x, and their outputs add up coordinate by coordinate. Before serving, you fold BA into W once (W = W0 + BA) and inference runs exactly like a normally fine-tuned model: no extra layers, no extra latency. Adapters cannot do this; they stay in the graph.'
+        ];
+        var timer = null, k = 0;
+        function clear() { for (var i = 0; i < btns.length; i++) btns[i].classList.remove('active'); }
+        function show(idx) { clear(); btns[idx].classList.add('active'); box.textContent = texts[idx]; }
+        function stopPlay() {
+            if (timer) { clearInterval(timer); timer = null; }
+            if (playBtn) { playBtn.textContent = 'Play'; playBtn.classList.remove('playing'); }
+        }
+        for (var j = 0; j < btns.length; j++) {
+            (function (idx) {
+                btns[idx].addEventListener('click', function () { stopPlay(); show(idx); });
+            })(j);
+        }
+        if (playBtn) playBtn.addEventListener('click', function () {
+            if (timer) { stopPlay(); return; }
+            /* Standing widget-layout rule: the How-to caption sits below the
+               tool, so bring it into view when Play starts. */
+            if (typeof playScrollEnsure === 'function') playScrollEnsure(demo);
+            k = 0; show(k); k = 1;
+            playBtn.textContent = 'Pause';
+            playBtn.classList.add('playing');
+            timer = setInterval(function () { show(k); k = (k + 1) % btns.length; }, 500);
+        });
+    })();
+    </script>
+
     <p>Now the numbers. Take a $768 \times 768$ attention projection, rank $r = 8$:</p>
     <ol class="walkthrough-steps">
       <li>$A$ is <strong>$8 \times 768 = 6{,}144$ parameters</strong>.</li>
